@page "/editor"

<CatHead Title="Rich-text editor chrome">
    The frame, the toolbar and the body for an editor your app brings — TipTap, Quill, ProseMirror or a
    plain <code>contenteditable</code>. Not an editor: the document model, the sanitiser and the
    security surface stay with the editor you chose.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.EditorChrome" Title="Around an editor" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — type in it.</strong> <code>.editor</code> is the frame and takes a focused field's
        ring while anything inside has focus. <code>.editor-toolbar</code> is a wrapping row of
        <code>.btn-ghost.btn-icon.btn-sm</code> with <code>.editor-sep</code> between groups, and a mark
        that is on is <code>aria-pressed="true"</code> — the attribute your editor already reports, which
        the toolbar colours. <code>.editor-body</code> is where the editor mounts and what scrolls, capped
        by <code>--editor-max-height</code>; add <code>.prose</code> to it so what is typed looks like
        what is later rendered. For Markdown, the library's own <a href="/markdown">editor</a> is the
        better fit.
    </p>
</CatExample>
